This answers the author's question about defaults: the diff enables `platform-certs` by default in `tauri-bundler` and `tauri-cli`. Every user then gets OS-trust-store TLS for all bundler and CLI downloads, not only users behind a proxy. Their trust roots change silently, and the default build compiles in `rustls-platform-verifier`, `jni` 0.19 (a second copy beside 0.21), and extra `security-framework` features.

An opt-in feature (off by default, documented in `ENVIRONMENT_VARIABLES.md` or the CLI docs) is the lower-risk choice. Alternatively, keep it on by default but state the trust-root change prominently in the changelog. The current changelog entry only says the bundler "will now read TLS Certificates installed on the system", which doesn't say the store replaces the bundled roots.

::diff{file="crates/tauri-cli/Cargo.toml" lines="140-149"}

The condition in `crate_version` changes from `lock.is_some() && crate_lock_packages.is_empty()` to `!crate_lock_packages.is_empty()`. The old branch effectively never set `lock_version` in the multi-entry path, so the fix is probably correct, but it changes `tauri info` output for every project with multiple lock entries of the same crate. The PR title and changelog say nothing about it, and no test covers it.

::diff{file="crates/tauri-cli/src/helpers/cargo_manifest.rs" lines="191-197"}

It should go in its own PR with a changelog entry and a test, or be dropped from this change.

The existing tests in `http_utils.rs` only cover `generate_github_mirror_url_from_template`. Nothing exercises `base_ureq_agent()`, the base-URL mirror (which the PR broke), the WebView2 download path, or the `crate_version` change. Any of these could regress without a failing test.

At minimum, add a unit test asserting that the base mirror appends the GitHub path (this would have caught the regression above). A `cfg(feature = "platform-certs")` test that builds the agent and checks it does not panic would be a cheap smoke test. Testing the actual cert behavior would need a local TLS fixture, which is optional here.

::code{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/utils/http_utils.rs" lines="56-73"}

When a GitHub URL is rewritten to a mirror, `generate_github_alternative_url` returns `ureq::agent()`, a bare default agent. That skips both the env proxy and the new platform verifier from `base_ureq_agent()`. Corporate users who need the mirror are exactly the ones who need the trust store, so the fallback path behaves differently from the rest of the PR.

::diff{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/utils/http_utils.rs" lines="42-49"}

Switch it to `base_ureq_agent()` so every bundler download uses one agent configuration.

`crate_latest_version` in the CLI duplicates the TLS builder from `base_ureq_agent()` in the bundler. It can't call that helper directly because it is `pub(crate)` in another crate. The two copies can drift, and the CLI copy already omits the proxy call the bundler version sets explicitly. It currently relies on ureq's default env-proxy behavior.

Expose one shared helper from `tauri-bundler` (or a small shared module) and use it from both places.

